vement that you get from being in an  airplane but you're gonna actually see  the forces  on their bodies as they pull g's  um flying in these uh amazing machines  you mentioned you have six cameras uh  and an f-18 cockpit is not very large  can you sort of talk about could talk  about the camera and how you can  actually put it in the cockpit  well it it the camera allows you to  separate the recorder from the lens so  uh by separating it the the lens is is  small enough that we figured out a way  to mount six of them in the cockpit and  hide the recorders  down in the cockpit where typically they  have other equipment so we had the navy  strip out all the equipment they didn't  need  uh to fly the plane and we stuffed  camera gear  um  in those places uh and if you look  really closely while you watch the movie  you'll see little camera wires running  along the edge of the cockpit which we  just left in there because we just  thought it was cool uh to see you know  the gear that we used to shoot the film  and what's funny is most people who are  going to watch it are just going to  think that's gear that's part of the  plane yeah yep exactly so that's why it  didn't bother me but it's it's proof  that we did shoot it for real uh did you  ever think you mentioned that uh the  dramatic scenes you wanted to be in the  in the normal screen did you ever think  about shooting the entire movie in imax  well we do capture the whole film full  frame so we have that ability  to open the whole film if we wanted to  but  um that extra headspace for those  dramatic scenes to me  uh didn't help you know it was good to  kind of keep it more intimate and then  open up the film for the um aerial  sequences so uh so that's the choice we  made so you're saying that when you're  shooting the movie in imax like this um  it doesn't change what you're doing on  set at all it's just a question of what  you're doing in the editing room yeah  it's when when do you want to open up  and use 